    <!-- ACT 1: the crisis at its worst. The object is not named. -->

    <h2 id="lived">The Man Who Wanted to Know Why He Lived</h2>

    <p>Most people picture a car crash the way they picture a hammer hitting a nail. A big heavy thing hits a smaller thing, and the smaller thing breaks. That is not what happens. In the great majority of fatal crashes, the car does exactly what it was built to do. The car stops. The killing happens in the half second after the car stops, when the people inside keep moving at the speed the car was doing a moment before. There is a name for that second event. Engineers call it the second collision. Almost nobody outside the field has heard of it.</p>

    <p>An American named Hugh DeHaven worked it out before almost anyone else, and he worked it out because he was in crashes and kept surviving them. He was born in Brooklyn in 1895. He tried to join the United States Army Air Corps in 1916 and was turned down, so he went to Canada and enlisted in the Royal Flying Corps, and while he was still training as a pilot his aircraft went down.</p>

    <p>He woke up in hospital with a ruptured pancreas and one question. Everything in that wreck had been crushed except his cockpit, the only part that held. He spent the rest of his life trying to understand why a body survives one crash and not another. In 1933 he was in a car crash as well, which only sharpened the question.</p>

    <p>Then he started dropping things. He put eggs on sleds and ran them into walls, because an egg, like a person, is a soft object inside a hard container, and he watched which arrangements of padding and restraint let the egg survive the stop. He wrote in one paper that people knew more about protecting eggs in transit than they knew about protecting human heads.</p>

    <p>By 1942 he had a research program at Cornell funded to study crash injuries, and by 1950 he had published the finding that reorganised the whole field. In a crash, there are two events, not one. The first is the vehicle hitting something. The second is the occupant hitting the vehicle. The injuries that kill people are overwhelmingly from the second event, and the second event is the one nobody was engineering for. DeHaven called the answer to it packaging. Cars, he argued, should be built to package the people inside them the way a crate is built to package a bottle.</p>

    <p>Here is the part that made people uncomfortable. If the killing force comes from the occupant moving after the car has stopped, then the fix is not softer roads or slower traffic or better brakes. The fix is to make the body stop moving at the same instant the car does.</p>

    <h2 id="toll">Fifty Thousand in One Year</h2>

    <p>In 1966, 50,894 people died on American roads. That is the death toll of a mid-sized city, in one year, in peacetime, spread over every state. The rate was 5.50 deaths for every 100 million miles driven, and 1966 was actually an improvement on the years just before it.</p>

    <p>Imagine the road in those years as an ordinary driver saw it. Most cars on a 1966 highway had two lanes and no centre barrier, so oncoming traffic closed on you at well over a hundred miles an hour. The steering column was a rigid steel shaft aimed at the driver's chest. The dashboard was metal or hard plastic, and the windscreen was ordinary glass that broke into blades.</p>

    <p>And most occupants were held in place by nothing at all. On the cars that did have something for the driver to fasten, roughly one person in ten used it.</p>

    <p>Imagine being a parent in that decade. You drive your children to school on a road where a head-on crash at forty miles an hour routinely kills everyone in one of the two cars. You have been told, by people who sound authoritative, that this is the price of the automobile, and that the only real safeguards are a careful driver and a little luck.</p>

    <p>This is the thing to hold on to before the rest of the story. It did not look like a solvable problem. It looked like weather. Every few years a city would count its dead, a governor would say something, and the number would go up again the following year.</p>

    <h2 id="second">The Crash After the Crash</h2>

    <p>Here is the physics, and it is worth getting right, because the whole story depends on it.</p>

    <p>You are sitting in a car doing fifty miles an hour. So is everything in the car, including you. Your body is moving at fifty, and the car is moving at fifty, and nothing is under any strain. Then the car hits a wall. The wall does not care about you. The car stops in about a tenth of a second, and the moment it stops, you are still doing fifty, because the only thing that was carrying you along was the car, and the car has just finished. The seat cushion decelerates with the car and slides out from under you. Physics has no opinion about where you would prefer to go.</p>

    <p>So you travel forward. At fifty miles an hour, an average adult crosses the gap to the steering wheel in a little under a tenth of a second, less time than it takes to blink, and hits it with the energy of a fall from the top of a four or five storey building. And then, if the door has come open, you keep going out into the road, or under the car.</p>

    <p>Pay attention to this number, because it is the answer hiding in plain sight. In the crash studies of that era, the thing that best predicted whether an occupant died was not how fast the car was going. It was whether the occupant was still inside the car when it stopped moving. Being ejected multiplied the odds of dying several times over. The car was not the killer. It was the only object on the scene that could have stopped you safely, and it had been built so that you slid past it into the scenery.</p>

    <p>So the problem reduced to a single engineering question, and once DeHaven and a handful of others had stated it that way, it was almost crude in its clarity. If the body travels forward after the car stops, then the body has to be caught. Not by luck, and not by the driver's reflexes, and not by a prayer. It has to be caught by something built into the car, every time, for a person who is asleep, or drunk, or screaming, or holding a child, and who has no chance to prepare.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: the wrong turns. The object is still not named. -->

    <h2 id="stomach">The Strap Across the Stomach</h2>

    <p>The idea of holding a person down in a moving vehicle is older than the car. The English engineer George Cayley, who built and flew gliders in the middle of the 1800s, fixed himself into his with a strap, and by 1928 every aircraft was required to carry one, because a plane that hits the ground does not stay upright and the passengers who are still attached have a chance.</p>

    <p>The first version that went into cars was simple: one strap, low across the waist. It did a real job. In a rollover, or a spin, or a low-speed shunt, it kept the occupant in the seat instead of on the road, and it stopped people going through open doors. It looked like the answer for a decade.</p>

    <p>However, the low strap had a flaw that only showed up in the hardest crashes, and it took doctors rather than engineers to see it. The strap crosses the middle of the body, which is the one part with almost no structure: liver, spleen, guts, and the front of the spine. Put several thousand pounds of force through that and it fails. Surgeons in the 1950s and 1960s began reporting a pattern of internal injuries that had clearly been caused by the restraint itself, and in some cases the spine separated at exactly the height of the strap.</p>

    <p>Then in 1949 an American carmaker, Nash, offered the low strap as a factory option. Forty thousand cars went out with it. The public response was not indifference. Buyers actively disliked it. A significant number asked their dealers to take it back out of the car. The feature that would eventually be credited with saving a million lives worldwide was, when it was first sold, a thing people paid to have removed.</p>

    <h2 id="clear">Better to Be Thrown Clear</h2>

    <p>Meanwhile, a belief had taken hold that would hold the field for years, and it was a belief about safety that was almost perfectly backwards.</p>

    <p>The argument went like this. If you are strapped in and the car catches fire, you burn. If you are strapped in and the car goes into a river, you drown. If you are not strapped in, the crash throws you clear of the wreckage, and a broken leg in a field beats a seat going up in flames. So the restraint was not a safety device at all. It was a trap.</p>

    <p>But the numbers said the opposite. Being thrown clear of a car is not surviving a crash. It is a second, uncontrolled impact with the road, a tree, a fence post or another vehicle, with no structure around you at all. In the crash data, ejection was one of the strongest predictors of death that existed. Fire and drowning, the two disasters the argument was built on, were rare causes of death compared with being launched from the car. People had protected themselves from the small danger and walked into the large one, because the small danger was easier to picture.</p>

    <p>Here is the honest part, because it matters to how the fix finally arrived. For years the car industry's position was not that restraints were dangerous. It was that restraints did not sell, and that it was not the manufacturer's job to force an unwanted product onto the market. There is a grain of truth in this, and it is the same grain of truth that killed people. Customers really did reject the early product. The mistake was concluding that the product was wrong, when the real problem was that it had never been made good enough or cheap enough to become a default.</p>

    <p>Then came the pressure from outside. In 1965 a young lawyer named Ralph Nader published a book attacking the American car as an unsafe product, and in 1966 the United States Senate held hearings on the same question. The hearings produced the first serious federal safety law for motor vehicles, and one of the things it did was to make restraint systems mandatory equipment in new cars. By 1968, every new car sold in the United States had to have a belt in the front seats.</p>

    <h2 id="start">The Year the Car Would Not Start</h2>

    <p>Here is where the story becomes a warning about what it takes to change a habit, and it is the reason this belongs in a series about objects rather than a series about laws.</p>

    <p>By the early 1970s, the device was in every new American car. The problem was that it was still being used by roughly one driver in ten. A belt only works if it is fastened, so the deaths kept coming at nearly the old rate. The engineers had solved the second collision on paper and had watched it kill people anyway, because the fix lived in the hands of a human being who could simply decline to use it.</p>

    <p>So the regulators tried force. For the 1974 model year, the United States required new cars to be fitted with a system that would not let the engine start until the driver had closed the restraint. Buckle up, or the car does not move.</p>

    <p>The backlash was immediate and total. Drivers hated being told by a machine that they could not start their own car, and the system was crude and failed often, which made it worse. Within months, Congress voted to ban the interlock outright, and the rule requiring it was dead. The lesson could not have been clearer: you could put the device in every car in the country, but you could not make a whole population use it by engineering away their choice. It has to be something they will actually accept.</p>

    <p>Even the intellectual case against the device was made in that same period. In 1975 an economist named Sam Peltzman published an argument that a restraint gives a driver a feeling of safety, that the feeling makes the driver more careless, and that the extra recklessness cancels out the protection. The general idea, that a safety device can change behaviour, has some support. The specific claim that restraint use cancels out its own benefits does not, and the data has gone the other way again and again. But the argument mattered, because it gave a respectable face to a feeling many drivers already had.</p>

    <p>So picture the state of the problem in the late 1950s, when the story really begins. A century of cars had taught the world that road deaths were inevitable. A theory of the second collision had explained exactly why people were dying. A basic restraint existed, was rejected by buyers, then mandated into every car, then ignored by nine drivers out of ten, and was now the subject of a national fight about freedom. Everyone could see the problem. The fix that would end it had not been designed yet, and the man who would design it was in Sweden, working on a completely different kind of crash.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: the fix arrives, still slightly oblique. -->
    <h2 id="jets">The Man Who Fired Pilots Out of Jets</h2>

    <p>Nils Ivar Bohlin was born on 17 July 1920 in Härnösand, on the Swedish coast, and finished a diploma in mechanical engineering in 1939. In 1942 he went to work for Saab, the aircraft maker, as a designer, and his speciality was one of the strangest problems in engineering: the ejection seat.</p>

    <p>Sit with that problem for a second, because it is the training that made everything afterwards possible. When a pilot leaves a supersonic jet, the seat is fired out of the aircraft by an explosive charge, and in the fraction of a second of the launch the body is accelerated at a force many times its own weight. The human frame can take that, but only if the load runs through the right parts in the right direction. So Bohlin spent the 1950s solving the exact problem Hugh DeHaven had named in 1950: how to subject a human body to a violent change in speed without letting it tear itself apart.</p>

    <p>In 1958 he was hired by Volvo as a safety engineer, and he was handed a prototype restraint and told to finish it. He worked on it for about a year. What he produced was not a bigger version of what already existed. It was a different shape, built around a single observation about the human body.</p>

    <h2 id="geometry">Two Strong Points and One Weak One</h2>

    <p>The observation was this. A body has two strong structures and one weak one. The strong ones are the ribcage, a cage of bone around the heart and lungs, and the pelvis, a ring of bone at the base of the spine. The weak one is everything in between, the soft abdomen. Every restraint designed before Bohlin had loaded the wrong part of that map. A single strap goes either high or low, and if it goes low it crosses the soft middle, and the soft middle fails.</p>

    <p>So Bohlin did not use one strap. He used two, built as a single unit. One ran across the chest, from the shoulder down to the opposite hip. One ran across the hips. Both met at one anchorage point low and to the side of the occupant, and a single buckle closed the whole thing. Look at what that geometry does. The chest strap takes the upper body and stops the head whipping forward. The hip strap stops the pelvis sliding under the chest strap, which is what lets a body fold in half in a crash. Between them the load spreads across both strong structures and skips the weak one.</p>

    <p>Then he added the part that mattered most, and it was not physics. The whole system had to close with one hand, in one movement, without the occupant looking, and it had to sit on the body comfortably enough that a person would leave it on.</p>

    <p>Bohlin explained the reasoning years later, and it is the one line worth hearing in his own words.</p>

    <p>"The pilots I worked with in the aerospace industry were willing to put on almost anything to keep them safe in case of a crash, but regular people in cars don't want to be uncomfortable even for a minute."</p>

    <p>Read that again, because it is the whole breakthrough in a sentence, and it is the opposite of what most people assume about safety engineering. An ejection seat could be uncomfortable, because a pilot would accept anything for the chance to survive. A driver would accept nothing. Ten years of mandates and laws had already proved that a restraint people would not wear protects nobody. So Bohlin designed the restraint around human vanity and human laziness, and it became the first one that people actually kept fastened.</p>

    <h2 id="proof">Twenty-Eight Thousand Crashes</h2>

    <p>The first car sold with the design as standard came off the line on 13 August 1959, and by 1963 every Volvo carried it. Then came the part that changed the law in other countries. In 1967 Bohlin presented a study to the Stapp Car Crash Conference, built on an analysis of 28,000 Swedish accidents. The findings were almost too clean. Occupants who were not held in place died across the entire range of crash speeds, from slow to fast. Occupants who were held in place were not killed below 60 miles an hour. And no occupant using the design was killed at any speed when the passenger compartment itself stayed intact. On his team's numbers the risk of injury or death fell by as much as 75 percent.</p>

    <p>Those figures were what persuaded the United States to require the three-point design in new cars, and persuaded other governments to follow. But the detail that makes this story different happened in a boardroom in Gothenburg. Volvo had patented the design, and then chose not to enforce the patent against anyone. The patent, filed in August 1959 and granted in 1962, was opened so that any manufacturer in the world could use the geometry for free.</p>

    <p>In an industry that fought its rivals over chrome and horsepower, that was close to unthinkable. Volvo's own reckoning, decades later, was that the design it gave away has saved at least a million lives worldwide.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: the reveal. -->

    <h2 id="chest">The Strap Across Your Chest</h2>

    <p>Now land it where it belongs, on the object you are wearing.</p>

    <p>So let us say it plainly. The thing that fixed the crisis in the first half of this story is the seat belt. Not a symbol of it. The same strap, over the same shoulder, down to the same point beside the same hip, closed by the same one-hand buckle that Bohlin worked out in a year of testing. The refinements since then are small: a reel that lets the belt move with you and locks when it is yanked, a tensioner that takes up the slack in the first instant of a crash, a force limiter that gives slightly so the belt itself does not injure you. What you are wearing is, in its essential shape, a sixty-seven year old piece of geometry.</p>

    <p>You fastened one today. You got into a car, sat down, reached up over your shoulder, pulled a strap across your chest, and pushed a metal tongue into a buckle until it clicked. It took about two seconds, and you did not think about it, the same way you did not think about it yesterday.</p>

    <p>Here is why that matters. In 1966, 50,894 people died on American roads in a single year, and nine out of ten of them were moving freely inside the car when it stopped. The fix for that was not a new hospital, a new drug, or a new law about how fast anyone was allowed to drive. It was a strap, on a hinge, that a person could close with one hand. You have been using it your whole life.</p>

    <h2 id="numbers">What It Bought, and What It Did Not</h2>

    <p>What has it actually bought? The honest answer is enormous and still incomplete, and the timeline is the point.</p>

    <p>Before 1980, American restraint use sat at about 11 percent despite decades of education campaigns, and by the end of a push in the early 1980s it had climbed only to about 15 percent. New York passed the first state law requiring use in 1984, and by 1990 thirty-seven other states had followed. That wave, and the enforcement campaigns after it, moved the national rate from 15 percent to 50 percent. Then a campaign that began in North Carolina in 1993, built on the blunt logic that a ticket is a better teacher than a poster, pushed it higher. By 2022, 91.6 percent of American drivers and front-seat passengers were using one.</p>

    <p>The result, in the numbers that count, is this. American road deaths per 100 million miles driven fell from 5.50 in 1966 to 1.19 in 2024. Restraint use is credited with saving about 15,000 American lives every year, roughly 14,955 in 2017 alone, and more than 315,000 between 1975 and 2013. Volvo's global estimate is past a million. A lap-and-shoulder restraint cuts the risk of a fatal injury to a front-seat car occupant by about 45 percent, which the Centers for Disease Control rounds to about half.</p>

    <p>Here is the honest part. In 2024, 22,713 people died in passenger vehicles in the United States, and nearly half of them were not wearing a restraint. The device works, and it only works if it is used, and a large minority of drivers still does not use it. That is the same gap the engineers hit in 1974, and it has never fully closed.</p>

    <p>And the problem is not finished anywhere. The World Health Organization counted 1.19 million road deaths worldwide in 2021. More than 90 percent of them happened in low- and middle-income countries, where the cars, the roads and the enforcement are all behind the standard that exists in Sweden and the United States. The fix that took thirty years to spread through one rich country has not finished spreading through the world.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: the lesson. -->

    <h2 id="pearl">The Part That Matters</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that matters.</p>

    <p>The reason this story belongs in a series about objects you use without thinking is the gap between how hard the problem looked and how simple the answer was. The crisis was 50,000 deaths a year and a public that believed it was unavoidable. The solution was a strap, given away, that costs almost nothing. Everything in between, a decade of engineering and then three more decades of laws and campaigns, was not the technical problem. It was the human one. The geometry was solved in a year. Getting people to use it took the rest of the century.</p>

    <p>There is a second lesson, and it is the one Bohlin saw before anyone else did. Every attempt before his had asked the human being to behave correctly. Learn to drive better. Stop taking risks. Accept a little discomfort to be saved. His design did the opposite. It assumed the occupant would be lazy, would be rushed, would wear whatever was comfortable, and would fasten the thing with one hand or not at all. He built for the person who would not cooperate, and that is exactly why it worked. You can find the same idea in the story of the bar you push to open a door, where the fix improved the door instead of improving the crowd, and you can read that one in <a href="/posts/exit-doors-would-not-open">the story of the panic bar</a>.</p>

    <p>That is what a solved problem feels like from the inside. It does not feel like a triumph. It feels like a two-second habit you have never once questioned, in a car you got into this morning, on a road that used to kill a city's worth of people every year.</p>

    <p>Here is the question worth carrying out of this one, and it is worth aiming at something you have already decided is too big. Somebody looked at the routine slaughter of ordinary people doing something ordinary, and decided it was not weather. It was a design flaw, and design flaws can be fixed. He fixed his with a strap and gave the idea away. The part that took thirty years was not the strap. It was us.</p>

