        <li>World Health Organization. "Road traffic injuries" fact sheet. (That an estimated 1.19 million people died from road traffic injuries in 2021, and that more than 90 percent of those deaths occurred in low- and middle-income countries.)</li>
        <li>Motor Vehicle Manufacturers Association v. State Farm Mutual Automobile Insurance Co., 463 U.S. 29, and the Federal Register of 31 October 1974. (That in 1974 Congress banned the ignition interlock after the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration had required a system preventing the engine from starting unless the restraints were fastened.)</li>
        <li>Peltzman, Sam. "The Effects of Automobile Safety Regulation," Journal of Political Economy, 1975, and subsequent reviews of the risk compensation literature. (That the general mechanism of risk compensation has some empirical support, while the claim of a complete offset of seat belt benefits is not supported by the evidence.)</li>
        <li>Wikipedia. "Ralph Nader" and "National Traffic and Motor Vehicle Safety Act." (That Nader's 1965 book <em>Unsafe at Any Speed</em> and the 1966 United States Senate hearings produced the first serious federal motor vehicle safety law, and that the three-point design became required equipment in new American cars by 1968.)</li>
        <li>Wikimedia Commons and the National Institute of Standards and Technology. "Seat belt testing." (Public domain test-sled photograph used as the hero image, no credit line required.)</li>
